  "query_preview": "(Today through Tuesday)...\nIssued at 1241 AM EST Wed Nov 4 2026\n\nA positively-tilted trough located over the Great Plains is slowly \ntilting more negatively this evening. At the surface, low pressure \ncentered over Minnesota is lifting northward with a trailing cold \nfront extending well southward through Iowa, Missouri, Arkansas, and \nthrough Texas. Surface low pressure is beginning to reform over \neastern Kansas into southwest Iowa as the base of the trough gains \nnegative tilt. This secondary low will then become the primary low \novernight, lifting northward into Wisconsin.\n\nHere in Indiana, the tightening pressure gradient from the low over \nIowa will help drive a potent 40-50kt low-level jet northward \ntonight. Strong warm air advection is ongoing with temperatures near \n70 as of 9pm. Surface winds have largely died down, compared to \nearlier, which is due to increasing boundary layer stability. ACARS \nsoundings out of IND show a rather deep stable layer, roughly to 3km \nagl, which will inhibit downward momentum transfer for the time \nbeing. Soundings also show a rather dry column with substantial \ndryness in the mid and upper-levels. As such, buoyancy is almost non-\nexistent in the current soundings.\n\nGoing forward, guidance is in good agreement showing greater moisture \nadvection in a narrow band ahead of the approaching cold front. This \nwill help improve the thermodynamic environment overnight from a \nconvective standpoint. Nevertheless, poor lapse rates and a stable \nboundary layer will limit instability to under 500 J/Kg MUCAPE. \nThunderstorms are still possible, however, due to limited CIN and \nsufficient large-scale forcing. Severe weather does not appear \nlikely, again due to poor lapse rates and limited instability. Gusty \nwinds are possible at least initially as rainfall encounters \nresidual dry air.\n\nShowers and embedded thunderstorms have entered our CWA form the \nsouthwest as of this writing (12am), progressing northeastward \nrapidly. High-resolution guidance shows a QLCS weakening it as it \nencounters the dry and stable pre-frontal environment. Gradually, as \nthe column becomes saturated, heavier showers and embedded storms \narrive later tonight and towards morning. Rainfall amounts between \n0.25 and 0.75 inches will be most common, with locally heavier \namounts possible further south and east.\n\nScattered showers may linger into the afternoon until the cold \narrives around 18z. After that, westerly surface winds and mostly \ncloudy skies are anticipated. Highs today will likely occur before \nsunrise.\n\nThursday and Friday...\n\nThursday looks quiet ahead of an approaching upper trough and \nsurface cold front. The trough and cold front will move through \nThursday night into Friday. Moisture will be limited with the Gulf \ncut off. Will have some mainly slight chance PoPs on Friday, with \nthe most coverage expected during the first half of the day.\n\nHighs on Thursday will be in the lower to middle 60s, but Friday \nwill see mainly upper 50s for highs as cooler air moves in behind \nthe front.\n\nSaturday onward...\n\nNorthwest flow will continue Saturday into early next week. An upper \nlow moving through southeast Canada will bring another cold front on \nSunday. Similar to Friday though, moisture will be very limited with \nthe Gulf cut off again. Will go with a dry forecast through the \nentire period.\n\nThe cold front won't have much cold air behind it. Highs in the \nmiddle 50s on Saturday will gradually moderate to the lower and \nmiddle 60s by Tuesday. Lows will be near freezing for parts of the \narea Saturday and Sunday nights.",
